WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.572
Welcome to Module 2, Designing Relational Databases.

00:00:03.572 --> 00:00:09.862
In this module, we are going to focus on the structure that makes reliable SQL and analytics possible later.

00:00:09.862 --> 00:00:16.577
Before we worry about writing long queries, we need to decide what each table represents, what one row means,

00:00:16.577 --> 00:00:21.042
how tables connect, and what rules the database should protect automatically.

00:00:21.042 --> 00:00:27.922
The examples use a small outpatient clinic, but the design ideas apply to many domains such as retail,

00:00:27.922 --> 00:00:29.982
education, finance, and operations.

00:00:29.982 --> 00:00:36.257
You will also see small PostgreSQL examples that run directly in the browser.

00:00:36.257 --> 00:00:40.452
When a code slide appears, run it here first, inspect the result, and if you want more practice,

00:00:40.452 --> 00:00:44.532
use Copy and paste the same SQL into the pgAdmin Query Tool.

00:00:44.532 --> 00:00:47.267
The goal is not to memorize a complete schema.

00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:54.102
The goal is to understand the design decisions behind the schema.

00:00:54.102 --> 00:00:58.617
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example, a clinic system separating patients,

00:00:58.617 --> 00:01:00.752
encounters, clinicians, and billing.

00:01:00.752 --> 00:01:09.777
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:01:09.777 --> 00:01:11.412
analyst can understand our work.

00:01:11.412 --> 00:01:16.647
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to defend later:

00:01:16.647 --> 00:01:21.577
what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:01:21.577 --> 00:01:24.427
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
